## Lost Limb Riders — Records and Record-Keeping Policy

**Document ID:** GOV-POL-002
**Document Title:** Records and Record-Keeping Policy
**Department:** Governance
**Document Type:** POL
**Version:** 1.0
**Effective Date:** August 12, 2026
**Review Date:** August 2027
**Document Owner:** Executive Director
**Approving Authority:** Board of Directors
**Supersedes:** None
**Related Documents:** GOV-POL-003 (Records Retention); GOV-POL-001 (Document Control); REC-MATRIX-001 (Retention Matrix); REC-REF-001 (Records Location Register); REC-SOP-001 (Storage and Protection); REC-SOP-002 (Destruction)
**Related Forms:** REC-CHK-001 (Annual Records Review Checklist)
**Record Classification:** Governance
**Retention Requirement:** Permanent (while in force)

---

## 1. Purpose

This policy defines what records the organization keeps, who owns them, where they live, and how they are protected. A record is the completed evidence that a transaction or activity actually occurred.

## 2. Record Categories

- **Governance records** — Articles of Incorporation, Bylaws, board minutes, resolutions, committee records, IRS determination letter.
- **Financial records** — ledgers, journals, bank statements, deposits, invoices, expense reports, payroll records, tax filings, grant and sponsorship records.
- **Personnel records** — employee files, payroll files, medical/accommodation files, I-9s, disciplinary and separation files, contractor records.
- **Program records** — participant files, waivers, program documentation, incident reports.
- **Transaction records** — the completed forms, registers, and approvals produced by this transactional layer.
- **Administrative records** — contracts, insurance policies, compliance filings, correspondence with lasting value.

## 3. Record Ownership

Each record category has a defined owner and custodian per REC-MATRIX-001. Ownership includes responsibility for accuracy, storage, access control, review, and authorized destruction.

## 4. Official Record Location

Official records are stored in the organization’s secure, encrypted, access-controlled records system, not in this public repository. See REC-REF-001 (Records Location Register) for where each record class belongs.

## 5. Completeness and Accuracy

- Records must be complete at the time of the transaction and updated when the transaction changes.
- Each record must be traceable to its transaction ID and cross-referenced to related records.
- No record may be altered without a documented correction trail showing who changed what, when, and why.

## 6. Confidentiality

- Restricted records (personnel, medical, payroll, donor, participant, credentials) are accessible only to authorized individuals on a need-to-know basis.
- Confidential records never appear in the public repository. Only blank templates and procedures do.
- Breaches are reported per the organization’s incident process (SAF-POL-002).

## 7. Access and Audit

- Authorized staff, board members, auditors, accountants, attorneys, and regulators may access records within their scope of duty.
- Records must be produced promptly in response to a proper audit or regulatory request (ADM-PROC-003).

## 8. Review and Destruction

Records are reviewed annually (REC-CHK-001) and destroyed only per the retention matrix (REC-MATRIX-001) and destruction procedure (REC-SOP-002). Destruction of confidential records uses secure destruction methods.

---

**End of Policy.**
